This week, Zambia’s second-term agenda moved from promise to parliamentary reality, with the President unveiling the Grow Zambia Agenda and securing major financial backing from the IMF and the US. Yet the week was also marked by a sobering reminder of the power of disinformation, as a false cash giveaway drew crowds and led to arrests, and by a mixed bag on the sporting front, with the Copper Queens facing an uphill battle for Olympic qualification.

## Main Stories

1. **Grow Zambia Agenda takes centre stage as Parliament opens** – President Hichilema officially opened the First Session of the 14th National Assembly, setting eight national targets under the 10-10-5-3-3-1-1-1 framework: 10 million tonnes of maize, 10,000MW of electricity, five million tourists, three million tonnes of copper, three million tonnes of soya beans, one million tonnes each of wheat and sugar, and US$1 billion in beef exports. The ambition is commendable, but the speech was light on the specific financing, timelines, and policy mechanisms needed to turn these numbers into reality – the nation will be watching for the budget and sectoral plans that must follow.

2. **IMF and US back Zambia with $1.5bn each** – The IMF has granted Zambia a new three-year financing arrangement of approximately $1.5 billion, disbursed every six months at zero interest, while Zambia and the US signed a $1.5 billion health MoU that will recruit 40,000 health workers over five years. These are significant votes of confidence, but the IMF’s conditions – building reserves, maintaining low inflation, and enhancing debt transparency – will test the government’s fiscal discipline, and the health deal’s implementation details, including the split between frontline and community workers, need close scrutiny.

3. **Police arrest 11 over false 'Miracle Friday' cash giveaway** – Police apprehended 11 people after a crowd gathered at National Heroes Stadium following disinformation circulated by former Petauke Central MP Emmanuel Jay Banda, who had promoted a "Miracle Friday" event promising cash distribution from a purported US$6 million fund. Police say the crowd was dispersed when they did not comply with orders. The incident is a stark illustration of how social media can be weaponised to create public disorder – and raises questions about how authorities balance public safety with the right to assemble, especially when the underlying claims are demonstrably false.

4. **Copper Queens face Olympic qualifier uphill battle** – Zambia lost 2-1 to Uganda in the first leg of the 2028 Los Angeles Olympic qualifier in Kampala, despite taking an early lead through Prisca Chilufya. Interim coach Charles Haalubono criticised the team’s lack of character and fighting spirit, while captain Barbra Banda insists the squad can overturn the deficit in the return leg in Ndola on October 13. The performance raises concerns about the team’s readiness for the high stakes of Olympic qualification, and the coaching situation remains unsettled with an interim coach at the helm.

## Other Notable Stories

**Politics and Governance:**
* The UPND has adopted Adrian Banda as its candidate for the Mandevu by-election on November 5, with former aspiring candidate Christopher Shakafuswa vowing to support him despite being "emotionally affected" by the decision. The by-election follows the attack on the constituency’s totalling centre in August.
* The Clerk of the National Assembly has announced plans for a new parliamentary chamber and expanded seating capacity, following a K39 million expansion to accommodate the increased number of MPs.

**Economy and Infrastructure:**
* The RTSA has approved bus fare hikes effective today, with intercity routes rising by 7% and local routes by K2-K3, following the recent fuel price increase of K6.17 per litre for petrol and K6.41 for diesel. The timing will add to the cost-of-living pressures on ordinary Zambians.
* Zambia and China have signed the Exchange of Notes for the construction of a new office building for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, deepening the bilateral relationship.

**Health:**
* Northern Province has achieved 97% viral suppression in its HIV response, with 62,781 of an estimated 67,334 people living with HIV virally suppressed. The province’s HIV prevalence stands at 12.9%, the second lowest in the country – a commendable milestone, though the remaining gap and new infections among youth remain concerns.

**Religion and National Observance:**
* The Church Mother Bodies have announced that December 29 will now be observed as the National Day of Prayer and Christian Heritage, merging the previous National Day of Prayer with the commemoration of Zambia’s declaration as a Christian Nation. The first observance will be held on December 29, 2026.

**Sports:**
* The Copper Princesses face China in a friendly in Casablanca today, ahead of the FIFA U17 Women’s World Cup, with the team targeting a podium finish. The tournament runs from October 17 to November 7.
* Zambia’s Chipolopolo dropped one place to 91st in the FIFA rankings despite a 10-point gain from their 2-0 win over Togo, as other nations made greater strides.
* Three schools – Kisasa, Kanzala, and Mukonzhi – booked quarter-final places via penalty shootouts at the FQM Trident Interschools Independence Tournament, which continues in Jiwundu.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* **Grow Zambia Agenda accountability:** Watch for the national budget and sectoral implementation plans that must give substance to the 10-10-5-3-3-1-1-1 targets – and for quarterly progress reports against these benchmarks.
* **IMF conditions and debt transparency:** Monitor whether the government meets the IMF’s requirements on reserve building, inflation control, and publication of debt statistics – and whether the $1.5bn health deal delivers the promised 40,000 health workers within the five-year timeframe.
* **Copper Queens return leg:** The October 13 reverse fixture against Uganda in Ndola is a must-win for Zambia to keep Olympic hopes alive – watch for team selection and whether the interim coaching situation is resolved.
* **Mandevu by-election:** The November 5 poll will be a test of electoral integrity following the August attack on the totalling centre – watch for the campaign tone and whether the ECZ ensures a free and fair process.